(version 1)
;; Freeze v2 worker/auditor/gate sandbox template (F16 / F19 / F21 / F26 / F31 / F35 / F36,
;; T29 / T38a).
;;
;; The sandbox is the ENFORCING barrier: the access audit still replays every static access it can
;; see, but a shell command can hide a path behind command substitution, `-c` code, base64 or
;; xargs, and no static analysis is complete.
;;
;; WRITES ARE A WHITELIST (F31). `(deny file-write* (subpath "/"))` denies every write in the
;; system, and the render block below re-allows exactly the launch directory, the fresh home and
;; the kernel devices. A deny-list over `(allow default)` left every system directory writable —
;; including the ones on the operator's PATH — so a launch could drop a binary that the driver
;; would run unsandboxed afterwards. Reads are a whitelist: the renderer re-allows the launch, cell, home,
;; interpreter, opencode and measured system paths.
;;
;; `render()` substitutes the single placeholder line below with the launch's rules:
;;   * the launch directory (run dir for the worker, audit dir for the auditor, one fresh copy for
;;     each acceptance gate) and the fresh per-launch home read-write;
;;   * every ancestor of the launch directory as a `literal` (opencode's config discovery calls
;;     realPath on them; the contents of an ancestor are still not readable, only its own node, so
;;     the directory NAMES of ancestors remain listable and are disclosed in README §3);
;;   * the interpreter and its prefixes readable;
;;   * `/dev/null`, `/dev/tty`, `/dev/fd` and a `/dev/ttysN` terminal writable — the character
;;     devices a shell and a test runner need, never a file under a system directory;
;;   * the cell and measured system paths read-only; other reads and writes are denied.
;;
;; SIGNALS (F35). `(deny signal)` confines kill(2) to the launch's own sandbox, so a worker cannot
;; signal the driver, the scheduler or another run; killing its own children still works.
;;
;; NETWORK (F26 / F36). `(deny network-outbound)` and `(deny network-bind)` close the network; the
;; render block re-allows exactly the driver's CONNECT-only allowlist proxy and this launch's own
;; loopback `opencode serve` port. No `remote unix-socket` rule exists, so unix-socket egress is
;; closed by omission, and `com.apple.dnssd.service` is denied, so a name cannot be resolved.
(allow default)
(deny file-read-data (subpath "/"))
(deny file-write* (subpath "/"))
(deny network-outbound)
(deny network-bind)
(deny signal)
(allow signal (target same-sandbox))
(deny mach-lookup (global-name "com.apple.dnssd.service"))
{{ALLOW}}
